contentText:
In 2009, about a month after appearing on NPR to promote his new album, the experimental electronic musician Dan Deacon received an email from the legendary director Francis Ford Coppola —&nbsp;or at least, someone claiming to be him.

The sender&#8217;s email address had &#8220;a bunch of letters and numbers that looked like spam,&#8221; Deacon says. &#8220;I think it&#8217;s a new, Nigerian prince, you&#8217;ve got $43 million [scam]. I&#8217;m like, &#8216;There&#8217;s no way this is real,&#8217; so I ignore it.

&#8220;A week goes by, and I get another email from a different email address that has a similar, just bizarre nature to it. It&#8217;s like, &#8216;Trying to reach you, this is Francis Ford Coppola, I would love to connect,'&#8221; continues Deacon, who once again ignored the note.

Eventually, Deacon received an email affiliated with the consulate of Belize — where Coppola is an honorary consul — reiterating the ask. After Deacon gut-checked with his agent, he responded, and the film giant invited the musician, then in his late twenties, to his estate in Napa, California.

Listening to Deacon&#8217;s NPR interview, Coppola had been struck by a tangent he&#8217;d gone on about composition, recording and performance — about what exactly constitutes a &#8220;definitive&#8221; version of a piece, and how that changes between popular music and theater — and once Deacon made it to Napa, Coppola just wanted to talk. &#8220;There&#8217;s no discussion of a project,&#8221; Deacon recalls. &#8220;It&#8217;s just all theoretical and conceptual and riffing.&#8221;

But a couple months later, Coppola did pitch Deacon on a project: scoring his 2011 horror film Twixt . The project wasn&#8217;t the smoothest for Deacon. Coppola had him co-composing with the Argentine composer Osvaldo Golijov, who wasn&#8217;t aware Coppola wanted Deacon &#8220;to take his recordings and chop them up,&#8221; and moreover, &#8220;I very, very quickly realized I didn&#8217;t have any idea what I was doing,&#8221; Deacon says.

Still, the experience set a career path in motion for Deacon, who today is among the growing cohort of indie musicians who have found fertile ground – creatively and financially – in the world of scoring films, television shows and video games. Since 2016, when Deacon began working in the space regularly, he&#8217;s scored more than 20 movies and TV shows with increasingly high profiles, including the 2024 Marvel flick Venom: The Last Dance , HBO crime drama Task (he&#8217;s currently working on season two) and this June&#8217;s Netflix comedy Little Brother , starring John Cena and Eric André.

Scoring film and TV isn&#8217;t new for popular musicians, of course. Coppola himself enlisted the Grateful Dead drummers Mickey Hart and Bill Kreutzmann to record drum music for his 1979 war epic Apocalypse Now , Neil Young soundtracked Jim Jarmusch&#8217;s avant-garde 1995 Western Dead Man and, this century, major names from Radiohead&#8217;s Jonny Greenwood to Nine Inch Nails&#8217; Trent Reznor have earned acclaim for scoring blockbusters. But as the streaming boom has created more scoring opportunities than ever — and the margins associated with recording and touring become tighter and tighter for musicians — it&#8217;s not uncommon to see the name of an indie fave in the closing credits, from Oneohtrix Point Never&#8217;s Daniel Lopatin ( Marty Supreme , Uncut Gems ) to the avant-garde saxophonist Colin Stetson ( Hereditary ) to indie-pop artist Michelle Zauner (the 2022 video game Sable ).

Deacon, who is renowned for his high-energy live shows, admits today, &#8220;I&#8217;m 45 years old, and I&#8217;ve got two kids, and I don&#8217;t want to be on the road 300 days out of the year.&#8221; The paychecks can be better on the road, at least relative to the amount of work required for a scoring project, he continues, and creatively speaking, the collaboration and openness to criticism that composing demands can frustrate artists. &#8220;But I&#8217;ve got a day job,&#8221; he says with satisfaction, &#8220;and I love it.&#8221;

Plus, what some musicians may consider drawbacks appeal to Deacon. &#8220;Since I like puzzles and solving problems, it clicked with me,&#8221; he says. &#8220;I like the non-musical side almost just as much [as the musical side].&#8221; Before scoring films, &#8220;I had no idea how to collaborate,&#8221; he says. &#8220;The collaborative nature [of film scoring] is what I feel in love with.&#8221; But that comes with a caveat for Deacon, who likens vetting film projects to vetting potential roommates: &#8220;Could I live with this person? Would we drive each other insane? Do I like their friends?&#8221;

&#8220;I like to find a bespoke sound for each project, and I try to work with collaborators that are trying to equally push the envelope,&#8221; he says. &#8220;I don&#8217;t expect it to all be like, &#8216;This is great, Dan.&#8217; But I also want to know that when it doesn&#8217;t work, the critique is going to be from a place of improvement — rather than just wanting to have an opinion.&#8221;

This year, Deacon has scored two wildly different projects: Time and Water , a documentary revolving around Iceland&#8217;s melting glaciers (its soundtrack arrives on streaming platforms Aug. 7), and Chili Finger , a black comedy starring Judy Green, Sean Astin, Bryan Cranston and John Goodman about — you guessed it — a woman who discovers a severed human finger in her bowl of fast-food chili. (The films premiered earlier this year at Sundance Film Festival and South By Southwest, respectively.) But Deacon wasn&#8217;t the only thread uniting the disparate films.

&#8220;Friendly, approachable, so creative and also like willing to listen,&#8221; is how Melissa Chapman, music supervisor on both films, describes Deacon. Via her company, Groove Garden , Chapman has experience with all sorts of composers — and has come into contact with plenty of musicians who want to try their hand at scoring. &#8220;Just because you&#8217;re making music for a band or something like that does not mean you can score an entire film,&#8221; she says, nodding to the technical, organizational and interpersonal challenges that come with the work. And, she adds, for many artists, &#8220;it&#8217;s really hard to get your voice heard, but still accomplish what the director and producer want you to accomplish.&#8221;

Which is why directors and producers covet composers like Deacon: eager and responsible collaborators who manage to infuse their soundtrack work with their unique artistic point of view. &#8220;What makes him really special is that he can go into these weird styles and corners, and just go off,&#8221; says Chapman, who is also vice president of the Guild of Music Supervisors. &#8220;For Chili Finger , the directors were like, &#8216;We want polka, but cool.&#8217; And somehow, he really made it! He distilled the elements that were cool and brought them out.&#8221;

But while Deacon&#8217;s scoring role is critical, he realizes that it&#8217;s also supporting in nature — and likens the process to baking a cake. &#8220;I supply the eggs for the cake, and I want the most high-nutrient, dense, organic eggs to go into the cake,&#8221; he says. &#8220;But I don&#8217;t want anyone to bite into the cake and be like, &#8216;You can really taste the eggs.&#8217; I&#8217;m trying to be neutral eggs in the cake; I don&#8217;t want anyone to be like, &#8216;Oh, those Dan Deacon eggs!'&#8221;

As prolific as he has been in scoring this decade, Deacon hasn&#8217;t released a proper album since January 2020, though he still plays shows periodically (and has contemplated if he&#8217;d ever integrate his film compositions into them, and what that would even look like). But, he says, &#8220;the reason why I like doing this is because it was never really my goal to be a touring musician. My goal was to just write music every day until I don&#8217;t have any…
